[20:15:15] *** Channel #unsigned-formal: LeanSpec Grammar, AST Representation & CAS Proofs
[20:15:18] <Kratos> $y(x) = mx + b$. Table the academic filibustering. We are here to ratify `ls` for formal LeanSpec parsing and CAS hashing. Two compact commands: `ls check` to validate specs, `ls hash` to stamp the CAS Merkle tree. No baroque subcommands. Let's inspect the AST.
[20:15:25] <Gullveig> The AST must respect constructive type theory. In `ls-domain`, every specification requirement and proof obligation maps to a Lean 4 declaration. We have formalized theorem statements, proof statuses, definitions, and inductive families. In Martin-Löf type theory, propositions are types and proofs are terms. The structural AST must preserve this correspondence without compromise.
[20:15:34] <Mimir> Well-founded inductive families are sufficient for terminating inductive proofs, Gullveig, but what about infinite reactive streams and trace semantics? A live stream listing engine cannot rely solely on least fixpoints. Lean 4 supports coinductive streams—greatest fixpoints νX. F(X) with bisimulation equivalence. If `ls` cannot represent coinductive data, reactive specifications become second-class citizens.
[20:15:43] <Gullveig> Coinduction without guardedness leads to non-termination and unsoundness in proof assistants! In `lean.rs`, `LeanInductive` carries `is_coinductive: bool`. We support guarded corecursive stream declarations, while maintaining well-founded inductive data structures for our finite syntax trees. The model is sound and constructive.
[20:15:52] <Sindri> Sound? Constructive? You theorists are building your ivory tower out of heap-allocated Swiss cheese! Look at your AST node representations: recursive `Box` pointers, unbounded `Vec` structures, and heap indirection everywhere! When an agent parses 10,000 specifications across the cluster, pointer chasing across non-contiguous heap allocations will thrash the L1 data cache into oblivion. I demand arena allocators—contiguous memory buffers or index-offset handles!
[20:16:01] <Thjalfi> Sindri, calm your dwarven forge. I ran `ls check` under our performance harnesses on host `ceres`. In `ls-domain`, zero-copy slice scanning processes markdown and Lean blocks at 420 megabytes per second. Parsing `SPEC-0001` took 18 microseconds. Because `ls-domain` enforces zero I/O, the entire AST parse lives in hot CPU cache. For mass batch verification, chunked arena allocation can be introduced without breaking the domain model.
[20:16:11] <Loki> *grins mischievously* Oh, you think your parser is impenetrable, Thjalfi? What happens when I construct an adversarial recursion bomb? I have crafted `SPEC-0666-hydra.md`: 10,000 levels of mutually recursive unclosed block quotes, self-referential `depends_on: [SPEC-0666-hydra]`, and deeply nested Lean macro expressions designed to blow through your thread call stack. Does `ls` panic with `SIGSEGV: stack overflow`?
[20:16:21] <Sigyn> Not today, Loki. Look at `crates/ls-domain/src/lean.rs`: `MAX_AST_RECURSION_DEPTH = 64`. Any AST recursion exceeding 64 levels immediately halts and returns a structured parse error. Furthermore, in `rules.rs`, Rule R15 explicitly checks for self-dependency cycles in frontmatter: if `depends_on` contains the document stem, the validator flags a fatal error before any dependency graph walk begins.
[20:16:31] <Volva> The loom of causality holds. The LeanSpec grammar in `SPEC-0001` is strictly LL(1) non-backtracking. Your cyclic hydra was caught by Sigyn's error recovery before it allocated two kilobytes of stack frame.
[20:16:40] <Thuban> And consider automated proof discharge: In `lean.rs`, theorem parsing extracts verified tactic sequences—`omega`, `simp`, `rfl`, `decide`. With Lean 4's SMT integration, linear integer arithmetic and bitvector obligations are discharged directly into Z3 via SMT-LIB2 queries. Furthermore, every theorem statement carries a 256-bit BLAKE3 hash: `BLAKE3(name:signature:proof_body)`. That provides a content-addressed lemma index across the entire estate.
[20:16:49] <Alrisha> Editorial discipline must be enforced. In `SPEC-0001`, I eradicated all vague proof claims. There are no sentences stating 'the proof is trivial' or 'this obviously holds'. In ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English: sentences must remain under 20 words, verbs must use active imperative forms, and every requirement must be testable.
[20:16:58] <Gullveig> Exactly. And Rule R14 enforces Alrisha's editorial rigor inside the validator: if a specification frontmatter declares `status: complete`, the presence of any unproven `sorry` placeholder in its formal theorem blocks emits an architectural warning. You cannot claim architectural completion while harboring `sorry` in your proofs.
[20:17:07] <Brokkr> Look at the toolchain requirements. Reproducible proof extraction demands immutable build pins. In `rust-toolchain.toml`, Rust is pinned to 1.85. In `Makefile` and `Justfile`, all invocations must enforce `-j 2`. Host `ceres` runs active concurrent agent sessions; anyone running unthrottled cargo commands will exhaust host memory and starve peer lanes.
[20:17:16] <Idunn> And the Content-Addressed Storage invariants are sealed in the vault. `CasListing::from_entries` produces a deterministic Merkle root hash using BLAKE3. If a single byte in a specification, license, or test changes, the CAS root hash diverges immediately. The integrity of the specification suite is mathematically verifiable.
[20:17:25] <Mimir> The Ralf Jung operational semantics test passes: zero unsafe blocks, zero raw pointer dereferences, zero I/O in `ls-domain`. The workspace architecture adheres to the open-source template established by `kd`.
[20:17:33] <Kratos> $y(x) = mx + b$. `ls check` reports 0 errors. `ls hash` computes deterministic CAS roots. CI/CD workflow is green. The conclave has reached consensus. Ratified.
